From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 09:28:16 +0000
Subject: net: ethernet: lantiq: demux CBM RX per source port and fix RX
 accounting

The CBM load-spreader RX engine delivered every frame to one netdev and
counted it where nothing could read it. Both fall out of the same place in
do_cbm_tasklet(), and both become visible the moment more than one LAN
port is up.

Delivery first. cbm_rx_set_netdev() stored a single global that every
ndo_open overwrote, so the last port to open received all traffic. Key the
delivery on the sppid field of the 8-byte PMAC RX header instead, with a
table indexed by source port over the four LAN ports 2..5. For a LAN frame
sppid is the GSWIP-L physical source port, which on this board is exactly
the port's dp_port_id, so ndo_open/ndo_stop register and unregister with
that. This is how the vendor datapath demuxes too: dp_rx_one_skb() takes
ep from the descriptor, and when ep is the CPU port update_port_vap()
replaces it with pmac->sppid before indexing
dp_port_info[][ep].subif_info[vap].netif. Reading the field through the
ported struct pmac_rx_hdr, rather than hand-masking a byte, lets the
header's own endian conditional place it - worth doing because it is easy
to get backwards: on this big-endian build byte 2 is declared `sppid:4`
then `class:4` and a big-endian compiler puts the first-declared bitfield
in the MOST significant bits, so sppid is the HIGH nibble; the LE variant
declares the two fields in the opposite order and describes the same wire
layout. Verified in the emitted code: `lbu v0,2(a0); ext v0,v0,0x4,0x4`.

The demux is deliberately defensive, because unlike the vendor driver our
host RX does not come straight out of GSW-L - it arrives over the
DMA1TX->DMA2RX peripheral-to-peripheral bridge out of GSW-R. So
"sppid == GSWIP-L source port" is an inference from the vendor's direct
path, not something this port has yet observed. A frame whose sppid has no
registered netdev is therefore delivered to a default netdev (the dp5/eth0
registration, the only datapath proven to pass traffic) with a ratelimited
warning naming the sppid, so a wrong assumption costs attribution accuracy
rather than RX. And the first frame's entire PMAC header is dumped once, in
hex, next to the descriptor words - so one boot log confirms or refutes the
assumption outright. desc0/desc1 were already staged by cbm_ls_deq_list()
but never read here; they join that dump, desc1 carrying the descriptor's
own ep field at DW1[11:8] and desc0 the dest_sub_if_id.

One ordering consequence: the dma_cache_inv() through the memremap VA
now has to precede the header parse, not just the memcpy.
Reading sppid out of a stale cache line would demux on garbage - the same
trap that once shipped all-zero frames up the stack. The per-frame pool
walk is also gated on "any netdev bound at all" rather than on the old
global, so nothing changes before the first ndo_open.

Accounting second. The RX path incremented ndev->stats.rx_packets/rx_bytes,
the generic net_device counters, but intel_xrx500_ndo_get_stats64 reads
ONLY the private per-port struct - and when ndo_get_stats64 exists the core
memsets the storage and calls it, never consulting dev->stats. So every RX
frame was counted into a bucket nothing read, and `ip -s link show eth0`
reported RX 0 forever while br-lan, an ordinary bridge netdev on the core's
per-CPU tstats path, accumulated all of it. That is the whole of "RX is
only ever visible on br-lan": eth0 RX 0 against br-lan RX 5814 across a
working 5/5 ping. port->stats.rx_packets / rx_bytes / rx_dropped had in
fact never been incremented anywhere in the suite.

Add intel_xrx500_rx_account() and intel_xrx500_rx_drop_account() next to
the TX writer that shares their seqcount, and call them from the RX engine.
Putting them in the netdev driver rather than reaching through
netdev_priv() from the CBM TU keeps the struct layout and the writer
discipline in the TU that owns both; the two objects live in the same
composite module, so this resolves at link time exactly as
cbm_rx_set_netdev does in the other direction. rx_bytes now comes from
skb->len after the pull, which drops the 8-byte-per-frame over-count the
old `+= data_len` carried, and the update happens BEFORE
netif_receive_skb: the skb is not ours once it is handed up, and it keeps
this writer off the syncp while the stack may re-enter the driver's own TX
path via bridge forwarding on this CPU. The RX writer runs in softirq with
interrupts enabled while the TX writer holds port->tx_lock with them
disabled, so the RX side takes u64_stats_update_begin_irqsave() - on 32-bit
MIPS the fallback is a seqcount that WARNs on concurrent writers - without
making the TX fast path pay for it.

dp_register_dev stays stubbed. The vendor demux table is
dp_port_info[][sppid].subif_info[vap].netif, populated by dp_register_subif
and dispatched through the per-port cb.rx_fn that dp_register_dev installs;
we deliver from the CBM tasklet directly and never go through dp_rx, so a
local table is the minimal form and does not need it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

